# sbt-riddl scripted test
# See: https://www.scala-sbt.org/1.x/docs/Testing-sbt-plugins.html

# Update dependencies
> update

# Download riddlc binary
> riddlcDownload

# Show version
> riddlcShowVersion

# Show build info
> riddlcInfo

# Validate via command wrapper
> validate

# Parse via task
> riddlcParse

# Prettify via command wrapper
> prettify

# --- Regression: sbt 2 result caching must not turn these tasks into no-ops ---

# Every riddlc task returns Unit, which sbt 2 CAN cache, while the files they
# actually read, the .riddl and .conf files, are discovered inside the body by
# resolveConfs and are therefore never declared as inputs. Without Def.uncached
# the second invocation is a cached no-op that still reports success, so
# riddlcValidate prints a success line and nothing else over a corpus that no
# longer validates. Editing sources, clean, and deleting target all fail to
# invalidate it, because none of them is an input that sbt knows about.

# Everything above this line runs each task exactly ONCE, which is why the bug
# shipped: a single invocation always executes. The shape that catches it is
# run, break, run again.

# First run populates whatever cache exists.
> riddlcValidate

# Break the model. Missing is undefined, so this fails with an ERROR rather
# than a warning. riddlc.conf sets hide-warnings, so only a real error can
# make riddlc exit non-zero.
$ copy-file changes/broken.riddl src/main/riddl/simple.riddl

# THE ASSERTION. This must fail. Before Def.uncached it returned a cached
# success and the broken model went unnoticed.
-> riddlcValidate

# Restore, and confirm the task recovers, proving the failure above came from
# the model rather than from a task left wedged.
$ copy-file changes/good.riddl src/main/riddl/simple.riddl
> riddlcValidate

# riddlcBastify deserves the same guard: it is what keeps committed .bast files
# in step with sources, so a cached success would leave a stale .bast looking
# freshly regenerated.

# Note the DIFFERENT fixture. bastify tolerates semantic errors: given
# broken.riddl, whose Missing path never resolves, it parses the model and
# writes a BAST with exit 0. Only a model that cannot PARSE makes it fail, so
# that is what proves this second invocation really ran.
> riddlcBastify
$ copy-file changes/unparseable.riddl src/main/riddl/simple.riddl
-> riddlcBastify
$ copy-file changes/good.riddl src/main/riddl/simple.riddl
> riddlcBastify
